# design.md Part 7.13's two v1 invariants, enforced instead of assumed.
#
# WHAT THE RULES ARE. Part 7.13 (`design.md:2618-2624`) ends with *"two
# rules, both already true, both now invariants"*. Reach the allocator
# through a **single point**, which §4.20 (`design.md:2210-2222`) names as
# `runtime/parts/alloc.c`; and keep `incref`/`decref` behind a **narrow,
# never-inlined boundary**, so that making the counter thread-local or
# atomic is ONE edit. Neither is tidiness. Isolated per-thread heaps are
# the concurrency model, and a second allocation site discovered later is
# a redesign rather than a patch.
#
# WHY IT IS A CHECK AND NOT A COMMENT. The first rule was **false to the
# letter** when somebody finally looked, and nobody had noticed for two
# days: design.md said one `malloc` and one `free` inside the `str`
# primitives, and the sweep found **eleven calls across five files**
# (`DESIGN-LOG.md:235`). That is what created `alloc.c`. Its own header
# then promised *"the invariant is checkable with one grep"* — and the
# grep was never written, so on 2026-09-05 `runtime/parts/array.c` was
# growing a static buffer with `realloc`, which is a second allocation
# site that the promised grep would not have seen anyway, because it
# names `malloc`.
#
# WHAT IS CHECKED.
#
# 1. `runtime/**` outside `runtime/parts/alloc.c` calls nothing from the
#    allocation family — `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`, `reallocarray`,
#    `aligned_alloc`, `strdup`, `strndup`, `free`. The family and not
#    `malloc` alone: `realloc` is what was actually there, and a rule
#    that names one spelling of allocation catches one spelling of the
#    mistake.
# 2. `runtime/heroes_runtime.h` carries no `inline`, and every line in it
#    that names an `incref` or a `decref` is a PROTOTYPE — `;`-terminated,
#    no body. The header is the one file the generated C includes, so
#    anything defined there is code clang may smear into a caller.
# 3. Every object in the runtime that survives between calls is
#    `_Thread_local`, or `_Atomic`, or written down in this file with the
#    reason it may stay shared and the milestone that will take it. Rule 3
#    arrived at M-isolated-threads step 3, and § Rule 3 below carries the
#    argument — including the five objects it found on its first run that
#    no count in this project had ever seen, because they live inside
#    function bodies and every count had looked at file scope.
#
# COMMENTS AND STRING LITERALS ARE NOT CODE, and this suite would be
# noise without that. `runtime/parts/run.c:484` contains the sentence
# *"the first check is free (WNOHANG, no sleep at all)"* — an English
# `free` followed by `(`, which a plain grep reports and a reader then
# learns to ignore. `code_lines` blanks every comment and every literal
# before a needle is looked for, and `alloc.c`'s own floor below is what
# proves it did not blank everything.

use report
use shell
use strings

constant RUNTIME: str
    "runtime"

# The one file allowed to call them, and the one place a per-thread heap
# will be built.
constant SINGLE_POINT: str
    "runtime/parts/alloc.c"

constant HEADER: str
    "runtime/heroes_runtime.h"

# The allocation family. C's `<stdlib.h>` and the two `<string.h>`
# duplicators, which allocate without looking like it.
constant ALLOCATORS: [str]
    [
        "malloc"
        "calloc"
        "realloc"
        "reallocarray"
        "aligned_alloc"
        "strdup"
        "strndup"
        "free"
    ]

# Floors, measured 2026-09-05: 21 files under `runtime/`, **4** allocation
# calls inside `alloc.c` (one `malloc`, three `free`), 6 refcount
# prototypes in the header. They exist for `suite_order`'s reason — a
# sweep that reaches nothing reports green — and the middle one is the
# load-bearing one here: it is what fails if `code_lines` ever blanks
# code as well as comments, which is the one way this suite could go
# quiet while the tree went wrong.
#
# **Floors, not equalities**, so a refactor that deletes a `free` does not
# turn this red; `suite_order`'s constants are set the same way. The
# parenthesis said *three (one `malloc`, two `free`)* for six hours, which
# is how long it took `hero_grow_kept` to add the fourth — CLAUDE.md §11's
# premise that expires in silence, inside the suite written to stop
# premises expiring in silence.
#
# And `alloc.c` holds a **fifth** occurrence, at its own line 24, inside a
# comment quoting `run.c`'s English *"the first check is free ("*. A grep
# reports five; `code_lines` reports four. That line is this suite's own
# reason living in the file it checks.
constant LEAST_FILES: i64
    18

constant LEAST_AT_THE_POINT: i64
    3

constant LEAST_REFCOUNT: i64
    6

## Rule 3 — every piece of shared mutable state is named

# WHAT THIS IS FOR. Part 7.13's model is isolated per-thread heaps, and
# what stands between the runtime and that model is not one thing: it is
# every object the C keeps between calls. Panel 111 measured four classes
# of memory corruption from one line of ordinary Heroes each, and three
# of them come from an object on the list below rather than from a
# refcount.
#
# WHY IT IS A CHECK AND NOT A TABLE IN A DOCUMENT, and this is the whole
# reason it exists. The number in the record is **22 mutable file-scope
# objects, 4 of them `_Thread_local`** (panel 111, 2026-09-05), and that
# number is right about what it counted. What it counted is FILE SCOPE.
# This sweep's first run, 2026-09-06, over the same 20 files: **28 mutable
# objects, and five of them live INSIDE a function** — `f64.c`'s cached C
# locale and four `static int done = 0;` once-guards. A `static` inside a
# function body is shared between threads exactly as much as one at the
# top of the file, and no count in this project had ever seen one.
#
# That is CLAUDE.md §1's newest rule met in the wild: the argument was
# sound and the LIST was short, and nothing in a correct argument points
# at the row nobody wrote down. So the list is taken from the tree on
# every run, and the allow-list below is the only thing that may be
# written by hand.
#
# **AND ON ITS SECOND RUN IT CAUGHT ITS OWN AUTHOR.** M-isolated-threads
# step 4 made ten of those objects `_Thread_local` and repaired the
# locale, and the same commit introduced FOUR new shared ones — a key, a
# slot and two once-guards, in the very hook written to give a thread's
# buffer back. The sweep named all four, an hour after it was written, in
# the run that was meant to confirm the ten. It also named the ten
# allow-list lines that had just stopped matching anything, which is the
# other direction and the one a hand-written table never notices.
#
# **AND A FOURTH TIME AT STEP 6**, which is the one worth reading,
# because it is the first entry on this list that is not waiting for a
# repair. `runtime/parts/spawn.c` gave the runtime its own way to start a
# thread, and its table of running threads plus the mutex guarding it are
# shared ON PURPOSE — a spawned thread may itself spawn, so two threads
# choose a slot at once, and choosing one is a read-then-write over
# several fields rather than one counter. That is the shape an atomic
# cannot make single, and it is `cow.c`'s shape; the difference is that
# here the runtime owns both sides, so a mutex is available and there it
# is not. The sweep named all three lines — the table and the lock in
# each platform arm — in the run that was meant to confirm the threads.
#
# Where it stands after step 6, measured that day: **35 objects across 21
# files, 15 `_Thread_local`, 3 `_Atomic`, 17 shared, 6 of them inside
# function bodies.** The 17 are the thirteen entries below, three of which
# cover a pair each; ten are `M-thread-stacks`' or are safe by where they
# are called, and two are the spawn table and its lock.
#
# WHAT IS ALLOWED. `_Thread_local` and `_Atomic` need no entry: the
# spelling itself says the object was thought about. Everything else must
# be named here with the reason it may stay shared, and the check fails in
# BOTH directions — an object nobody named is red, and an entry that names
# nothing is red too, so the day one of these becomes thread-local its
# line here has to go.

# `<path> <name> | <why it may stay shared>`. One entry covers every
# object of that name in that file, which is what lets the four `done`
# once-guards be two lines: `os.c` has two and `stack.c` has two, one in
# each of its platform arms, and each pair shares the one reason.
constant SHARED_BY_DECISION: [str]
    [
        "runtime/parts/alloc.c hero_kept_key | the POSIX key whose destructor gives a thread's kept buffer back. Written ONCE, inside `pthread_once`, and only read afterwards — which is what a once-guard buys and the one shape where `shared` and `safe` are the same word. A plain `_Atomic` flag would not do: two threads racing to make the key would make two, and the loser's destructor would never run"
        "runtime/parts/alloc.c hero_kept_slot | the Windows half of the same thing, `FlsAlloc`'s index, written once inside `InitOnceExecuteOnce`. Windows has no pthreads at all — measured on the box 2026-09-06, `pthread.h` is not found under clang targeting MSVC"
        "runtime/parts/alloc.c once | the two once-guards themselves, `pthread_once_t` and `INIT_ONCE`, one per platform arm. Being shared is their entire job and the platform owns their synchronisation; there is nothing here for this project to make safe"
        "runtime/parts/spawn.c hero_spawn_slots | THE ONE OBJECT IN THIS RUNTIME THAT IS SHARED ON PURPOSE AND GUARDED BY A LOCK, and the only entry here that is not waiting for a repair. It is the table of running threads, and it cannot be any of the three other answers: a spawned thread may itself spawn, so two threads choose a slot at the same moment, and choosing one is a read-then-write over several fields rather than one counter — the shape an atomic cannot make single, which is `cow.c`'s shape and the reason panel 113 left that open. Here the answer is a mutex because here the runtime owns both sides of it (design.md Part 7.13; panels 111, 113, 114)"
        "runtime/parts/spawn.c hero_spawn_lock | the mutex that guards the table above, one per platform arm — `pthread_mutex_t` on POSIX, `SRWLOCK` on Windows, which has no pthreads at all. Being shared is what a lock IS, and the platform owns its safety"
        "runtime/parts/os.c done | two once-guards, for byte-mode stdout on Windows and for unbuffered stderr. Safe because of WHERE THEY ARE CALLED and not because of how they are written: both run inside `hero_args_set`, which the generated `main` calls before a program's first line, on the thread that owns the program. If a second caller is ever added this entry is wrong"
        "runtime/parts/os.c hero_argc | the process's own argument count, written once by `hero_args_set` before any thread can exist and only read afterwards. Process-wide by nature: `args()` must answer the same thing on every thread"
        "runtime/parts/os.c hero_argv | the process's own argument vector, and the same reasoning"
        "runtime/parts/spawn.c hero_spawn_floor | the stack of the thread that ran `main`, which every thread this runtime starts is given at least as much of (panel 115). Written ONCE by `hero_args_set` before any thread can exist and only read afterwards, which is `hero_argc`'s shape two entries above — and process-wide BY NATURE rather than pending a repair: it is a fact about the process, so a per-thread copy would be a different and wrong answer on the second thread that spawns a third"
        "runtime/parts/stack.c hero_stack_prev_segv | the disposition installed before this runtime's, so a fault it does not own is passed on. Shared BY THE KERNEL'S OWN DEFINITION and not while waiting for a repair: `sigaction` sets a handler for the whole process, where `sigaltstack` and the bounds are the calling thread's, and reading that split the other way is exactly what made this guard silent on every thread but one until M-thread-stacks"
        "runtime/parts/stack.c hero_stack_prev_bus | the same, for SIGBUS"
        "runtime/parts/stack.c done | the once-flag over the PROCESS-wide half of the guard — the two dispositions on POSIX, the vectored handler on Windows — in each platform arm. The per-thread half moved out from under it at M-thread-stacks and is `_Thread_local`, so what is left here is genuinely the process's; `hero_args_set`'s callee, exactly like `os.c`'s pair above"
    ]

# A floor rather than an equality, on `LEAST_FILES`' reason: what would
# make this check pass while the tree went wrong is a sweep that reads
# nothing, and 28 is what it read on 2026-09-06.
constant LEAST_SHARED_STATE: i64
    24

function run_all(scratch: str) -> report.Report?
    nothing: [str] = []
    files = shell.list_tree(scratch: scratch, dir: RUNTIME, skip: nothing)?
    sources: [str] @ []

    for path in files
        if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".c") || strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".h")
            sources @ sources.push(path)

    r: report.Report @ report.empty()
    keep_single_point(@r, sources: sources)
    keep_boundary(@r)
    keep_shared_state(@r, sources: sources)
    return ok(r)

# Rule 1: every allocation in the runtime happens in one file.
function keep_single_point(@r: report.Report, sources: [str])
    strays: [str] @ []
    at_the_point: i64 @ 0

    for path in sources
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(@r, label: "runtime/alloc", detail: "  cannot read " + path)
            continue
        rows = code_lines(text.must())
        at: i64 @ 0

        while at < rows.len()
            row = strings.trimmed(rows[at])

            if row.len() == 0
                at @ at + 1
                continue

            for name in ALLOCATORS
                if calls(code: row, name: name)
                    if path == SINGLE_POINT
                        at_the_point @ at_the_point + 1
                    else
                        strays @ strays.push("    " + path + ":" + (at + 1).to_str() + ": " + row)

            at @ at + 1

    if strays.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "runtime/alloc",
            detail: "  design.md Part 7.13 makes the allocator a SINGLE POINT, `" + SINGLE_POINT + "`, because isolated per-thread heaps need one place to change and a second allocation site is a redesign rather than a patch (design.md:2210, :2582). These calls are somewhere else:\n" + strays.join("\n") + "\n  Route each one through `" + SINGLE_POINT + "`: `hero_alloc_block`/`hero_release_block` for memory the LANGUAGE owns and the leak gate counts, `hero_alloc`/`hero_release` for a buffer borrowed and given back inside one runtime call, `hero_grow_kept` for one the runtime keeps for the life of the process."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

    if sources.len() < LEAST_FILES || at_the_point < LEAST_AT_THE_POINT
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "runtime/alloc",
            detail: "  the sweep read " + sources.len().to_str() + " files and found " + at_the_point.to_str() + " allocation calls inside " + SINGLE_POINT + ", and had " + LEAST_FILES.to_str() + " and " + LEAST_AT_THE_POINT.to_str() + " — either it is not reaching the tree, or `code_lines` is blanking code and not only comments, and then the check above passes by seeing nothing"
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# Rule 2: the refcount boundary is narrow and cannot be inlined.
function keep_boundary(@r: report.Report)
    text = read_file(HEADER)

    if text.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "runtime/refcount", detail: "  cannot read " + HEADER)
        return
    rows = code_lines(text.must())
    wrong: [str] @ []
    declared: i64 @ 0
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < rows.len()
        row = strings.trimmed(rows[at])
        where = "    " + HEADER + ":" + (at + 1).to_str() + ": " + row

        if names_word(code: row, name: "inline")
            wrong @ wrong.push(where)

        if counts(row)
            declared @ declared + 1

            if !is_prototype(row)
                wrong @ wrong.push(where)
        at @ at + 1

    if wrong.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "runtime/refcount",
            detail: "  design.md Part 7.13 keeps `incref`/`decref` behind a NARROW, NEVER-INLINED boundary, so that making the counter thread-local or atomic is one edit rather than a hunt through everything clang smeared it into (design.md:2220, :2584). " + HEADER + " is the one file the generated C includes, so it holds declarations and no code:\n" + wrong.join("\n") + "\n  Put the body in `runtime/parts/`, beside the others, and leave the `;` here."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

    if declared < LEAST_REFCOUNT
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "runtime/refcount",
            detail: "  the sweep found " + declared.to_str() + " refcount declarations in " + HEADER + " and had " + LEAST_REFCOUNT.to_str() + " (a str, an array and a map, increfed and decrefed) — it is not reading the header"
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# Rule 3: no object survives between calls in the runtime without
# somebody having decided whether a second thread may see it.
function keep_shared_state(@r: report.Report, sources: [str])
    unnamed: [str] @ []
    seen: {str: bool} @ {}
    total: i64 @ 0

    for path in sources
        if !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".c")
            continue
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(@r, label: "runtime/threads", detail: "  cannot read " + path)
            continue
        rows = code_lines(text.must())
        at: i64 @ 0

        while at < rows.len()
            row = strings.trimmed(rows[at])

            if !declares_object(row)
                at @ at + 1
                continue
            total @ total + 1

            # The spelling itself is the decision, so these need no entry.
            if names_word(code: row, name: "_Thread_local") || names_word(code: row, name: "_Atomic")
                at @ at + 1
                continue
            key = path + " " + object_name(row)

            if named_here(key)
                seen[key] @ true
            else
                unnamed @ unnamed.push("    " + path + ":" + (at + 1).to_str() + ": " + row)
            at @ at + 1

    if unnamed.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "runtime/threads",
            detail: "  design.md Part 7.13 is isolated per-thread heaps, so an object the runtime keeps between calls is either per thread, or atomic, or a decision somebody wrote down (design.md:2618). These are none of the three:\n" + unnamed.join("\n") + "\n  Make it `_Thread_local` if it is scratch for one walk, `_Atomic` if it is a count the whole process shares, or add it to `SHARED_BY_DECISION` in this file with the reason it may stay shared and the milestone that will take it."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

    stale: [str] @ []

    for entry in SHARED_BY_DECISION
        wanted = entry_key(entry)

        if seen[wanted].is_err()
            stale @ stale.push("    " + wanted)

    if stale.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "runtime/threads",
            detail: "  these entries of `SHARED_BY_DECISION` matched nothing in the tree:\n" + stale.join("\n") + "\n  An entry that names nothing is a premise that has expired in silence (CLAUDE.md §11) — the object was renamed, moved, or has become `_Thread_local` or `_Atomic` and no longer needs an excuse. Delete the line, or fix the `<path> <name>` it names."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

    if total < LEAST_SHARED_STATE
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "runtime/threads",
            detail: "  the sweep found " + total.to_str() + " objects that survive between calls and had " + LEAST_SHARED_STATE.to_str() + " — it is not reaching the tree, and then the check above passes by seeing nothing"
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# An object that survives between calls, at file scope OR inside a
# function body — the second is the half every count in this project had
# missed, and `f64.c`'s cached locale is why it is swept.
#
# `const` is left out because it cannot be written to, and a line holding
# `(` is a function: a prototype, a definition, or a body on one line.
# **That last one is a premise about the world and it is written as a
# falsifiable claim** (CLAUDE.md §11): a function POINTER at file scope
# would be an object this reads as a function and skips. There are none
# on 2026-09-06, measured; the day one arrives, this sentence is what is
# wrong, and the `LEAST_SHARED_STATE` floor is what will not notice.
function declares_object(row: str) -> bool
    if !strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "static")
        return false

    if row.len() < 8
        return false

    if word_byte(row[6])
        return false

    if !strings.ends_with(text: row, suffix: ";")
        return false

    if names_word(code: row, name: "const")
        return false

    return !strings.contains(text: row, needle: "(")

# The declared name: the last identifier before whatever ends the
# declarator — `=`, `[` or the `;` itself. Walking back from there is
# what gets `hero_dir_names` out of `static char **hero_dir_names` and
# `hero_run_words` out of an array declaration.
function object_name(row: str) -> str
    cs = row.chars()
    stop: i64 @ cs.len()
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < cs.len()
        c = cs[at]

        if c == "=" || c == "[" || c == ";"
            stop @ at
            break
        at @ at + 1

    i: i64 @ stop - 1

    while i >= 0 && !word_byte(cs[i][0])
        i @ i - 1

    last = i + 1

    while i >= 0 && word_byte(cs[i][0])
        i @ i - 1

    return cs.slice(from: i + 1, to: last).join("")

function entry_key(entry: str) -> str
    cut = strings.index_of(text: entry, needle: " | ")

    if cut.is_err()
        return strings.trimmed(entry)

    return strings.trimmed(entry.slice(from: 0, to: cut.must()))

function named_here(key: str) -> bool
    for entry in SHARED_BY_DECISION
        if entry_key(entry) == key
            return true

    return false

# Does this line declare a refcount entry point? `counts` is the general
# half and `hero_str_incref` above is the literal one, so a rename that
# drops the project's own `incref`/`decref` vocabulary still trips the
# floor rather than passing quietly.
function counts(row: str) -> bool
    return names_word(code: row, name: "incref") || names_word(code: row, name: "decref") || strings.contains(text: row, needle: "_incref") || strings.contains(text: row, needle: "_decref")

function is_prototype(row: str) -> bool
    trimmed = strings.trimmed(row)
    return strings.ends_with(text: trimmed, suffix: ";") && !strings.contains(text: trimmed, needle: "{")

## The reader

# A line of `text` per line of source, with every comment and every
# string or character literal blanked to spaces. Positions are kept, so a
# failure still names the column a reader sees in their editor.
#
# It walks characters rather than bytes because `chars()` is the one
# spelling that cannot split a multi-byte character, and the runtime's
# comments are English prose with the odd non-ASCII dash in them.
#
# A raw newline inside a literal ends the row and leaves the state on,
# which would blank the rest of the file. C forbids one, so clang refuses
# the runtime before this suite ever reads it — and `LEAST_AT_THE_POINT`
# is the guard that fires if it ever happens anyway.
function code_lines(text: str) -> [str]
    cs = text.chars()
    out: [str] @ []
    row: [str] @ []
    block: bool @ false
    line: bool @ false
    quoted: bool @ false
    charred: bool @ false
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < cs.len()
        c = cs[at]
        after = char_at(cs, at: at + 1)

        if c == "\n"
            out @ out.push(row.join(""))
            row @ []
            line @ false
            at @ at + 1
            continue

        if block
            row @ row.push(" ")

            if c == "*" && after == "/"
                row @ row.push(" ")
                block @ false
                at @ at + 2
                continue
            at @ at + 1
            continue

        if line
            row @ row.push(" ")
            at @ at + 1
            continue

        if quoted || charred
            row @ row.push(" ")

            if c == "\\" && after != "\n"
                row @ row.push(" ")
                at @ at + 2
                continue

            if quoted && c == "\""
                quoted @ false

            if charred && c == "'"
                charred @ false
            at @ at + 1
            continue

        if c == "/" && after == "*"
            row @ row.push(" ")
            row @ row.push(" ")
            block @ true
            at @ at + 2
            continue

        if c == "/" && after == "/"
            row @ row.push(" ")
            row @ row.push(" ")
            line @ true
            at @ at + 2
            continue

        if c == "\""
            quoted @ true
            row @ row.push(" ")
            at @ at + 1
            continue

        if c == "'"
            charred @ true
            row @ row.push(" ")
            at @ at + 1
            continue
        row @ row.push(c)
        at @ at + 1

    out @ out.push(row.join(""))
    return out

function char_at(cs: [str], at: i64) -> str
    if at < 0 || at >= cs.len()
        return ""

    return cs[at]

## The needles

# Is `name` CALLED here? A whole word, followed by `(` across any spaces.
# The word boundary is what tells `malloc(` from `hero_malloc_raw(`, and
# the `(` is what tells a call from a mention.
function calls(code: str, name: str) -> bool
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at + name.len() <= code.len()
        if matches(text: code, at: at, needle: name) && !joined_before(text: code, at: at) && opens(text: code, at: at + name.len())
            return true
        at @ at + 1

    return false

# Is `name` here as a whole word at all, called or not? What the `inline`
# ban needs: `static inline int f(void)` opens no parenthesis of its own.
function names_word(code: str, name: str) -> bool
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at + name.len() <= code.len()
        if matches(text: code, at: at, needle: name) && !joined_before(text: code, at: at) && !joined_after(text: code, at: at + name.len())
            return true
        at @ at + 1

    return false

function matches(text: str, at: i64, needle: str) -> bool
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < needle.len()
        if text[at + i] != needle[i]
            return false
        i @ i + 1

    return true

function joined_before(text: str, at: i64) -> bool
    if at == 0
        return false

    return word_byte(text[at - 1])

function joined_after(text: str, at: i64) -> bool
    if at >= text.len()
        return false

    return word_byte(text[at])

function opens(text: str, at: i64) -> bool
    i: i64 @ at

    while i < text.len() && (text[i] == ' ' || text[i] == '\t')
        i @ i + 1

    return i < text.len() && text[i] == '('

function word_byte(b: u8) -> bool
    return b >= 'a' && b <= 'z' || b >= 'A' && b <= 'Z' || b >= '0' && b <= '9' || b == '_'

test "prose that says free is not a call, and a comment hides a real one"
    source = "/* the first check is free (WNOHANG, no sleep at all) */\nvoid f(void) { /* free(p); */ }\n"
    rows = code_lines(source)
    assert rows.len() == 3
    assert !calls(code: rows[0], name: "free")
    assert !calls(code: rows[1], name: "free")

test "a string literal is not code either"
    rows = code_lines("hero_panic(\"malloc(3) failed\");\n")
    assert !calls(code: rows[0], name: "malloc")
    assert calls(code: rows[0], name: "hero_panic")

test "the check fires on the shape that was actually in the tree"
    rows = code_lines("        HeroEqWork *grown = (HeroEqWork *)realloc(hero_eq_queue, cap * sizeof(HeroEqWork));\n")
    assert calls(code: rows[0], name: "realloc")
    assert !calls(code: rows[0], name: "malloc")

test "a longer name that ends in an allocator is not one"
    rows = code_lines("    void *p = hero_malloc_raw(size);\n")
    assert !calls(code: rows[0], name: "malloc")
    rows2 = code_lines("    void *p = malloc(size);\n")
    assert calls(code: rows2[0], name: "malloc")

test "reallocarray is its own needle, because realloc stops at the a"
    rows = code_lines("    p = reallocarray(p, n, size);\n")
    assert !calls(code: rows[0], name: "realloc")
    assert calls(code: rows[0], name: "reallocarray")

test "a character literal cannot open a comment or hide one"
    rows = code_lines("    if (c == '/') { free(p); }\n")
    assert calls(code: rows[0], name: "free")

test "the refcount check fires on a body and passes on a prototype"
    assert is_prototype("void hero_str_incref(HeroStr s);")
    assert !is_prototype("static inline void hero_str_incref(HeroStr s) { s.p->rc++; }")
    assert counts("void hero_array_decref(HeroArrayHeader *a);")
    assert !counts("void hero_array_new(const HeroDesc *elem, int64_t len);")

test "the inline ban reads a word and not a fragment"
    assert names_word(code: "static inline int64_t hero_len(HeroStr s)", name: "inline")
    assert !names_word(code: "    /* inlined */ int64_t hero_inlineness;", name: "inline")
    assert !names_word(code: "void hero_str_incref(HeroStr s);", name: "inline")

test "an object that survives between calls is one, and a function is not"
    assert declares_object("static int64_t hero_live_blocks = 0;")
    assert declares_object("static char **hero_dir_names = NULL;")
    assert declares_object("static struct sigaction hero_stack_prev_segv;")
    assert declares_object("static char *hero_run_words[HERO_RUN_MAX_ARGS + 1];")

    # A prototype, a one-line body and a definition are all functions.
    assert !declares_object("static void hero_drop_array(void *elem);")
    assert !declares_object("static void hero_drop_nothing(void *elem) { (void)elem; }")
    assert !declares_object("static int hero_dir_walk(const char *root, const char *prefix,")

    # A table nobody can write to needs no decision.
    assert !declares_object("static const HeroDesc hero_desc_array = {sizeof(HeroArrayHeader *)};")

    # And a word that merely starts with the six letters is not the keyword.
    assert !declares_object("staticky_thing = 0;")

test "the swept object is found inside a function body too"
    # The half every count in this project had missed. `f64.c`'s cached
    # locale is this shape, and it is reached by printing a float.
    assert declares_object(strings.trimmed("    static hero_locale cached = HERO_NO_LOCALE;"))
    assert declares_object(strings.trimmed("    static int done = 0;"))

test "the name is the last identifier before what ends the declarator"
    assert object_name("static int64_t hero_live_blocks = 0;") == "hero_live_blocks"
    assert object_name("static char **hero_dir_names = NULL;") == "hero_dir_names"
    assert object_name("static char *hero_run_words[HERO_RUN_MAX_ARGS + 1];") == "hero_run_words"
    assert object_name("static struct sigaction hero_stack_prev_segv;") == "hero_stack_prev_segv"
    assert object_name("static _Thread_local bool hero_drop_running = false;") == "hero_drop_running"
    assert object_name("static int done = 0;") == "done"

test "an allow-list entry is read up to its reason, and every one names something"
    assert entry_key("runtime/parts/os.c hero_argc | the process's own count") == "runtime/parts/os.c hero_argc"
    assert entry_key("runtime/parts/os.c done") == "runtime/parts/os.c done"
    assert named_here("runtime/parts/os.c hero_argc")
    assert !named_here("runtime/parts/os.c a_name_nobody_wrote")

    # This line read `runtime/parts/f64.c cached` for the hour between step 3
    # and step 4, and step 4 went red on it: the entry went when the locale
    # stopped racing. A witness picked from a list that is meant to SHRINK is a
    # test with an expiry date, so this one names an object whose entry says it
    # is process-wide by nature rather than one that is waiting for a repair.
    assert !named_here("runtime/parts/f64.c cached")
